No. The answer turns on the distinction between what can be taught and what must already be present. The speaker’s practical claim is: $\text{skills can be taught}
  \qquad\text{but}\qquad
  \text{traits cannot be reliably taught.}$ This should be read as a hiring judgment, not as a universal theorem about human beings. Inside this interview, it means that a founder may be able to train a person into a role, but cannot safely assume that humility, coachability, and persistence will appear after the offer letter is signed.

